        <body>
            <div xml:id="d116">

                <pb facs="0284" n="160" xml:id="pg160"/>

                <head>No. 116.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Martinez to Mr. Hitt</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of Chili</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Washington, D.
                                C.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1881-05-04"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 4,
                    1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Esteemed Sir and Friend</hi>: The New York papers are
                    publishing a rumor that it is possible that Chili may establish a protectorate
                    over Peru, because that country cannot form a government of its own, and because
                    many sensible people are asking for such a protectorate.</p>
                <p>I am sorry that I cannot call and speak with Mr. Blaine personally on this
                    subject. Nevertheless I desire to inform him, through you, that there is no
                    ground whatever for such rumor; and that, even though <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >all</hi> the Peruvians should clamor for a protectorate, Chili would on no
                    account consent to establish one.</p>
                <p>With sentiments of highest esteem, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>M. MARTINEZ.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
